<p>London police said they arrested 43 people at rival demonstrations on Saturday: Tommy Robinson&#x27;s “Unite the Kingdom” march and a pro-Palestinian Nakba Day rally combined with an anti-racism march organised by Stand Up to Racism. The Metropolitan Police deployed more than 4,000 officers to keep the groups apart, using a “sterile zone” and other public-order measures in one of the force&#x27;s largest operations in years. Police said 20 arrests were linked to Unite the Kingdom, 12 to the Nakba Day march and 11 were unrelated or not yet tied to either event. The Met said 11 arrests involved suspected hate crime offences, including nine linked to Unite the Kingdom and two linked to the Nakba march; seven other suspected hate crimes at the pro-Palestinian protest remained under investigation. The Met estimated about 60,000 people joined the Unite the Kingdom march and 15,000 to 20,000 attended the Nakba Day rally, while organisers of the pro-Palestinian march estimated about 250,000 attended. Police reported no serious clashes, though four officers were assaulted and six were targets of hate crime offences during the operation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/8WwiLnLi_ix4tM_sRm3e6FzetKwxaIfKBl5ekcYo8XjOF6P0BcrTnsOW1g2jp4zrVRQOO6G308SPxqI_SbRVUzdBQJ2UClIEdOVBRf_uPO6E' alt='People with masks of US President Donald Trump, Israel&#x27;s PM Benjamin Netanyahu, Jeffrey Epstein, Britain&#x27;s former PM Tony Blair, Reform UK Leader Nigel Farage and Justice Secretary David Lammy as demonstrators from the Palestine Solidarity Campaign hold a march to mark the 78th anniversary of the Nakba and against the “Unite the Kingdom” rally in London, May 16, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Hate offences: The Met said alleged hate crime offences involved race, religion, sexuality and disability.</li><li>Face coverings: At the Nakba Day demonstration, police arrested three people accused of failing to remove a face covering.</li><li>Security measures: Police used armored vehicles, horses, dogs, drones and helicopters as part of the central London security plan.</li><li>Lower turnout: The Met said the Unite the Kingdom crowd was less than half the size of Robinson&#x27;s September 2025 London event.</li></ul><blockquote>Immigration’s the main concern - Christine Turner</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Metropolitan Police: The force said it feared clashes because of violent confrontations at Robinson’s September rally and incidents at pro-Palestinian demonstrations since 2023. (<a href='https://www.newarab.com/news/uk-police-detail-arrests-after-far-right-rally-counter-demo'>The New Arab</a>)</li><li>Keir Starmer: Before the demonstrations, the British prime minister accused organisers of the Unite the Kingdom march of “peddling hate and division, plain and simple.” (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330459089/tens-of-thousands-march-in-london-in-separate-immigration-propalestinian-protests'>Aaj TV</a>)</li><li>Tommy Robinson: Robinson told supporters at the rally to prepare for a “battle of Britain” and posted video messages aimed at Starmer during the protest. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/may/16/tommy-robinson-tells-tens-of-thousands-at-london-rally-to-prepare-for-battle-of-britain'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Pro-Palestinian organisers: Organisers of the Nakba Day march estimated turnout at around 250,000 and said the event opposed Israel&#x27;s war in Gaza and Robinson&#x27;s far-right rally. (<a href='https://www.newarab.com/news/over-250000-join-nakba-day-march-against-gaza-war-far-right'>The New Arab</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260517-uk-police-make-43-arrests-at-far-right-rally-counter-pro-palestinian-demo-london'>UK police make 43 arrests at far-right rally, counter pro-Palestinian demo</a> - rfi.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/may/17/arrests-unite-the-kingdom-rally-pro-palestine-march-london'>Police a